---
name: nls-java
description: NLS Java coding conventions for .java changes, including dependency injection, naming, null-handling, comment rules, and JUnit testing patterns.
---

## Types and null-handling

- Never return `null`; return `Optional<T>` for a possibly-absent single value and an empty collection for absent lists/sets/maps.
- Use `var` for local variables only when the type is obvious from the right-hand side.
- Use records for immutable DTOs and value objects.
- Use generics everywhere; never use raw types.
- Prefer `List`/`Set`/`Map` interfaces over concrete implementation types in signatures.

## Naming

- Classes/interfaces/enums/records: `PascalCase`.
- Methods and variables: `camelCase`, full words, no abbreviations.
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`, declared `private static final`.
- Boolean names: `isX`, `hasX`, `canX`.
- Packages: lowercase, dot-separated, no underscores.

## Imports

- Absolute imports only; never wildcard imports.
- Order imports in groups separated by a blank line: `java.*`/`javax.*`, then third-party (`org.*`, `com.*`, `io.*`, alphabetical), then local application packages.
- Never use static imports except for constants and test assertion methods.

## Comments

- Keep comments minimal; code should be self-documenting through clear naming and small methods.
- Do not add comments in tests.
- Never write comments that explain obvious code behavior; extract a well-named method instead.
- Never document parameters/behavior in comments; use Javadoc and precise types instead.
- Never use section-separator comments inside a method; extract code into functions.
- Acceptable comments: complex algorithm explanations, framework/API workarounds, non-obvious business rules, and `TODO`/`FIXME` markers.
- Add Javadoc for public API methods and non-obvious service methods; skip it for simple getters/setters and self-explanatory methods.

## Error handling

- Throw specific, custom exceptions from service/business logic; never swallow exceptions in an empty catch block.
- Never catch generic `Exception` and return `null` or a default value silently; rethrow, wrap, or log and rethrow.
- Handle/translate exceptions at the boundary (controller/handler layer), not deep inside business logic.
- Log with a class-level `private static final Logger LOGGER` and parameterized messages (`LOGGER.info("Creating {}", id)`), never string concatenation.

## Formatting

- Always use braces, even for single-statement `if`/`for`/`while` blocks.
- One blank line between methods; one blank line after package/import blocks.
- Keep line length within the project's configured limit; wrap long parameter lists and chained calls for readability.

## Class structure

- Order members: static constants, instance fields, constructors, public methods, package-private methods, private methods, static methods.

## Testing

- Use JUnit 5 (`org.junit.jupiter.api.Test`); use module-level test methods, avoid unnecessary nested classes.
- Do not add comments in test code.
- Use descriptive test names in the form `methodUnderTest_condition_expectedResult`, plus a `@DisplayName` with a natural-language description.
- Mock all external dependencies with Mockito (`@Mock`, `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)`); construct the system under test manually with those mocks via its constructor.
- Prefer AssertJ (`assertThat(...)`) over plain JUnit assertions.
- Test one behavior per test method; verify interactions with `verify(...)` only when a side effect matters.
- Use fixture/test-data-builder helper methods or classes for reusable test data instead of duplicating object construction.
